1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,018
Pulse tests and regressions. Is the difference real, and what drives it? Taken apart in depth.

2
00:00:06,818 --> 00:00:13,525
Tests and regressions read the raw records that survive the filters, during the same scan as everything else.

3
00:00:14,325 --> 00:00:19,581
Post-tests are the second tier. They run after windows, on the finished result rows.

4
00:00:20,381 --> 00:00:26,746
Each test names a type and a field, and often a split. A regression names a target and its predictors.

5
00:00:27,546 --> 00:00:36,493
Results land in their own sections of the response, each carrying its own details. Our cohort here has two hundred and forty illustrative rows.

6
00:00:37,293 --> 00:00:47,626
Every test returns the same shape: the statistic, its degrees of freedom, a two-sided p-value, the alpha it was judged against, and whether the null was rejected.

7
00:00:48,426 --> 00:00:53,682
The details carry the rest, like the difference, its confidence interval and an effect size.

8
00:00:54,482 --> 00:00:59,311
Read both. A tiny p-value with a tiny effect is still a tiny effect.

9
00:01:00,111 --> 00:01:06,348
Start from the outcome. A number across two groups, or three or more. The same subjects measured twice.

10
00:01:07,148 --> 00:01:12,105
A yes or no rate, two categories, two numbers, or the shape of a distribution.

11
00:01:12,905 --> 00:01:18,140
Skewed data pushes you toward the rank tests, and unequal spread toward the Welch forms.

12
00:01:18,940 --> 00:01:25,967
Here is spend for arm A and arm B. The t-test asks whether the difference between their means is bigger than chance.

13
00:01:26,767 --> 00:01:34,498
It uses Welch's form, so it never assumes equal spread. B spends about seven more, and the effect size is moderate.

14
00:01:35,298 --> 00:01:43,519
With skewed values, Mann-Whitney compares ranks instead. It asks a different question, so disagreement is information, not a bug.

15
00:01:44,319 --> 00:01:48,807
With three or more groups, one-way ANOVA asks whether any of the means differ.

16
00:01:49,607 --> 00:01:57,551
Here it says no: region explains about one percent of spend. A rejection would only say that some means differ, not which.

17
00:01:58,351 --> 00:02:03,650
Check normality per group and equal spread first. Those checks are evidence, not proof.

18
00:02:04,450 --> 00:02:14,933
Unequal spread calls for Welch's ANOVA, skew for Kruskal-Wallis. To find which pairs differ, run Tukey as a post-test with the ANOVA's within-group mean square.

19
00:02:15,733 --> 00:02:21,096
Chi-square tests whether two categorical fields are independent, here region and arm.

20
00:02:21,896 --> 00:02:25,275
It compares each count with what independence would predict.

21
00:02:26,075 --> 00:02:32,419
The p-value says the pattern is unlikely by chance, but Cramér's V says the association is weak.

22
00:02:33,219 --> 00:02:40,566
When an expected count falls under five, Pulse warns. For a small two-by-two table, use Fisher's exact test.

23
00:02:41,366 --> 00:02:45,790
Pearson's r measures how tightly two numbers move together in a straight line.

24
00:02:46,590 --> 00:02:50,438
Spend and visits correlate strongly, at about point seven eight.

25
00:02:51,238 --> 00:02:57,710
Pearson only sees straight lines, and outliers dominate it. Spearman and Kendall use ranks instead.

26
00:02:58,510 --> 00:03:06,518
And a correlation of group means can even reverse the raw one. That's Simpson's paradox, so the result says which rows it used.

27
00:03:07,318 --> 00:03:12,723
Post-tests run last, on the result rows: after groups, aggregations and windows.

28
00:03:13,523 --> 00:03:20,699
The trend test asks whether an ordered series is rising or falling. Running it on a moving average tames the noise.

29
00:03:21,499 --> 00:03:29,870
Tukey compares every pair of group means while controlling the family-wise error. Result rows carry no weights, so it never runs weighted.

30
00:03:30,670 --> 00:03:38,059
Run many tests and some will look significant by luck. Add a multiplicity block, and Pulse corrects them as a family.

31
00:03:38,859 --> 00:03:42,899
The adjusted p-value sits beside the raw one, which never moves.

32
00:03:43,699 --> 00:03:50,022
Holm sorts the p-values, multiplies the smallest by four, the next by three, and keeps them in order.

33
00:03:50,822 --> 00:03:57,401
Pick a method and a family. If ten or more p-values go uncorrected, predict raises an advisory.

34
00:03:58,201 --> 00:04:03,798
Ordinary least squares fits a straight line through the data. Here, spend from visits and tenure.

35
00:04:04,598 --> 00:04:11,305
Each residual is the gap between a point and the fit. Least squares makes their squares as small as possible.

36
00:04:12,105 --> 00:04:21,201
Each coefficient comes with a standard error and an interval. Each extra visit adds about three point one five to spend, holding tenure fixed.

37
00:04:22,001 --> 00:04:29,945
It explains about seventy percent of the variation. A null in any column drops that row, and the fit streams in a single pass.

38
00:04:30,745 --> 00:04:36,705
The same fit covers many textbook names. Penalties give ridge, lasso and elastic net.

39
00:04:37,505 --> 00:04:42,484
Resampling replaces the standard errors with jackknife or bootstrap estimates.

40
00:04:43,284 --> 00:04:51,868
Selection searches the predictors by AIC or BIC. A dropped predictor vanishes from the output: absent is not zero.

41
00:04:52,668 --> 00:04:58,436
For a curve, build polynomial columns upstream as features, then fit a straight line through them.

42
00:04:59,236 --> 00:05:04,385
For a yes or no outcome, the generalized linear model fits a logistic curve.

43
00:05:05,185 --> 00:05:12,873
Its coefficients are on the log-odds scale. Each extra visit multiplies the odds of converting by about one point four.

44
00:05:13,673 --> 00:05:19,526
It always buffers, because the fit iterates. Perfectly separated data never converges.

45
00:05:20,326 --> 00:05:24,537
The Bayesian linear fit starts from a prior and updates it with the data.

46
00:05:25,337 --> 00:05:32,150
It reports credible intervals and no p-values at all. With a vague prior, it lands near the least-squares answer.

47
00:05:32,950 --> 00:05:38,547
Tests built on running counts and moments stream, and so do least squares and the Bayesian fit.

48
00:05:39,347 --> 00:05:44,326
Rank tests, exact tables, iterative fits, resampling and selection buffer.

49
00:05:45,126 --> 00:05:51,363
A mean-based test beside a Welford aggregate on the same field reuses its running state for free.

50
00:05:52,163 --> 00:06:00,342
That's the toolkit today: tests that compare, relate and check shape, multivariate tests, and regression engines with their modifiers.

51
00:06:01,142 --> 00:06:08,745
More are added over time, and embedders can register their own tests. The manifest always shows what your instance offers.

52
00:06:09,545 --> 00:06:14,438
Remember: read the effect size beside the p-value. Next up, matrices.

53
00:06:15,238 --> 00:06:17,081
Pulse tests and regressions.
